  "query_preview": "/Through Sunday/...\nIssued at 124 PM MDT Sun Aug 30 2026\n\nCurrent radar shows storms are making their way off the northern \nFront Range early this afternoon, with another round moving across \nthe Continental Divide. The primary hazard with today's showers and \nstorms will, once again, be strong outflow winds. So far, storms \nhave been producing a few tenths of rainfall and wind gusts between \n40 to 50 mph. Storms have been moving between 30-40 kts, which will \nhelp keep flash flooding concerns limited, but if we do see any back-\nbuilding, there is enough moisture in place to see some heavy \ndownpours, mainly across the higher elevations. While today's \ninstability is expected to be slightly increased over Saturday, it\nremains fairly weak, with only a few hundred to roughly 500 J/kg \nof MLCAPE observed on the SPC mesoanalysis. CAMs continue to be in\ndecent agreement about high-based storms/showers continuing their\ntrek eastward across the plains through the afternoon and \nevening. These will be capable of producing strong outflow winds, \nwith at least a few severe wind gusts possible. Current \ntemperature readings show roughly a ten degree difference from \nthis time yesterday, with today's afternoon highs forecast to run \na few degrees cooler than yesterday. \n\nSouthwesterly flow aloft will continue to usher monsoonal moisture \ninto Colorado on Monday. Given the increased moisture, proximity to \nthe right entrance of a 500 mb jet, and the aid of lift from an \napproaching shortwave sometime in the afternoon/evening, this will \nbe our best chance for precipitation across the forecast area \nwith this wave of monsoonal moisture. CAMs continue to show a \ncluster of storms move through the Four Corners Monday morning, \npushing northeast across Colorado through the day. We could see a \nfew stronger to severe storms in the afternoon, with the CAMs \nhoning in on the better instability being along and east of the \nPalmer Divide (as high as ~1200 J/kg of MLCAPE). Given the better \nmoisture, we will have a better chance for some localized heavier \nrainfall amounts, but with the mean flow aloft roughly moving \n35-40 kts, this should help limit flash flood potential. \nAdditionally, with DCAPE ranging from 1200-1500 J/kg across the \nplains, generally east of the urban corridor, the primary threat \nwill be with gusty outflows once again, but with increasing 0-6 km\nbulk shear, we may see some marginally severe hail with some of \nthe stronger storms. Depending on the timing of the shortwave, we \ncould see showers/storms linger late into the night. Near normal \ntemperatures are expected across the forecast area. \n\nSouthwesterly flow aloft will remain in place through the week as \nthe upper-level ridge persists over the eastern US. Monsoonal \nmoisture is expected to continue flowing into the region, but \namounts are expected to diminish for a few days midweek, as the \nhigher amounts should stay to our southeast. Things in the Pacific \nlook to become active over this period, from a parade of storms \nmoving towards Hawaii, to a fairly strong closed low dropping south \nalong the West Coast. Depending on how these features interact, we \ncould see some more active weather return by late in the week, but \nfor now, the forecast calls for slightly less convection each \nafternoon after Monday, with near normal temperatures on Tuesday, \ngradually warming through the end of the work-week.",
